\chapter{The Preprocessor and Optional Lua}
\label{ch:preprocessor}

A preprocessor is a source-to-source pass. It runs over a piece of text,
recognises a small vocabulary of directives, rewrites what it recognises,
and hands the result to whatever comes next --- a lexer, a parser, or a
human being. Every general-purpose programming language has one, and every
domain-specific language eventually wishes it had one too, because a
language that has to be recompiled in order to change a configuration
constant is a language that is annoying to use.

DSLtk's preprocessor is deliberately small, and its smallness is the point.
It implements object-like and function-like macros, the six conditional
directives, \texttt{\#define}, \texttt{\#undef}, \texttt{\#include}, \texttt{\#error}
and a scripting hook, and it does all of that in about five hundred lines.
What it does \emph{not} do is touch the filesystem or embed a scripting
language. Both of those are delegated to the user through a pair of
\code{std::function} members, and the library will not work at all until
they are supplied.

That division is worth dwelling on, because it is the design principle that
runs through the whole of DSLtk. The library provides \emph{mechanism}; the
embedding application provides \emph{policy}. A preprocessor that opened
files itself would be unusable in a language server, in a build tool that
wants virtual files, or in a sandbox. A preprocessor that embedded Lua
would drag a scripting language into every program that used DSLtk. By
exposing \code{std::function<std::string(std::string_view)> include} and
\code{std::function<std::string(std::string_view, std::string_view)> script},
the preprocessor stays a few kilobytes of header code while remaining
usable in every one of those settings.

The consequences of the policy separation are visible in the details. The
resolver receives the path text of an \texttt{\#include} with its delimiters
removed, and it cannot tell whether the original was written with quotes or
with angle brackets --- the library does not distinguish them, because the
only consumer of the answer is the user's resolver, and a resolver that
wants to distinguish them can be given a path that encodes the difference.
Likewise the scripting hook is asked for a language name and a body of code
and must return a string; the library does not care which language that is,
and in fact the only hook that ships with DSLtk is a Lua one, which is
compiled in only when the user defines \code{DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA}.

\section{A worked example}

The program below defines a few macros, supplies an in-memory include
resolver, and preprocesses a source string containing an include, two
conditionals, a function-like macro, a comment and a string literal. It
prints the result.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Preprocessing a source string with macros, conditionals and an in-memory include.},label={lst:ch34-preprocess}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>

int
main ()
{
  dsl::Preprocessor pp;

  // Object-like and function-like macros.
  pp.define ("NAME", "world");
  pp.define_function ("SQUARE", { "x" }, "((x) * (x))");
  pp.define ("VERSION", "3");

  // An in-memory include resolver.  The library never touches the
  // filesystem; it just hands the path to this function.
  std::map<std::string, std::string> files{
      { "config.h", "#define ENABLED 1\n" },
      { "greeting.h", "greeting NAME\n" },
  };
  pp.include = [&files] (std::string_view path) -> std::string
    {
      auto found = files.find (std::string (path));
      if (found == files.end ())
        throw std::runtime_error ("unknown include: " + std::string (path));
      return found->second;
    };

  std::cout << "---- expand only ----\n";
  std::cout << pp.expand ("SQUARE(NAME) and NAME\n");

  std::string source =
      "#include \"config.h\"\n"
      "#include \"greeting.h\"\n"
      "#if VERSION >= 3 && defined(ENABLED)\n"
      "int value = SQUARE(4);\n"
      "#elif VERSION == 2\n"
      "int value = 2;\n"
      "#else\n"
      "int value = 0;\n"
      "#endif\n"
      "#ifdef ENABLED\n"
      "const char *mode = \"enabled\";\n"
      "#endif\n"
      "#undef NAME\n"
      "// a line comment mentioning NAME\n"
      "const char *who = \"NAME is not expanded here\";\n";

  std::cout << "---- processed ----\n";
  std::cout << pp.process (source);

  std::cout << "---- an unknown directive ----\n";
  try
    {
      pp.process ("#pragma once\n");
    }
  catch (const std::exception &error)
    {
      std::cout << "caught: " << error.what () << '\n';
    }

  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch34-preprocess}.},label={lst:ch34-output}]
---- expand only ----
((world) * (world)) and world
---- processed ----
greeting world
int value = ((4) * (4));
const char *mode = "enabled";
// a line comment mentioning NAME
const char *who = "NAME is not expanded here";
---- an unknown directive ----
caught: unknown preprocessor directive pragma
\end{lstlisting}

Five things in that output are worth pointing out, because each of them is a
behaviour that a naive implementation would get wrong.

The \texttt{\#include "config.h"} directive produces \emph{nothing} in the
output. The included text is a \texttt{\#define}, and a \texttt{\#define}
produces no output; it only changes the macro table. The macro
\code{ENABLED} is nonetheless defined afterwards, which is why the
\texttt{\#ifdef ENABLED} branch was taken.

The \texttt{\#if VERSION >= 3 \&\& defined(ENABLED)} condition is true, and it
is true because \code{VERSION} is an object-like macro whose replacement
\code{3} is evaluated as an expression --- not because the preprocessor
knows anything about version numbers.

The function-like macro \code{SQUARE(4)} expands to \code{((4) * (4))}. The
argument is substituted into the replacement text and the result is
expanded again, so a macro whose body mentions another macro works as
expected.

The line comment mentioning \code{NAME} is copied verbatim, and so is the
string literal. This is the single most important property of a
preprocessor that operates on arbitrary text: a comment is not text, and a
string literal is not text. A macro named \code{NAME} must not be expanded
inside either of them. The implementation achieves this by handling
comments and quoted strings before it ever looks for an identifier.

Finally, \texttt{\#pragma} is not a directive this preprocessor knows, so it
raises \code{"unknown preprocessor directive pragma"}. That is the honest
behaviour for a directive that is not implemented: an error rather than
silence.

