The two entry points are not redundant. \code{expand} is the pure macro
expander, and it is what a user wants when the macros are known and the
text is not a program --- in a documentation generator, or in a build
system that wants to resolve constants in a configuration file.
\code{process} is the full pass, and it is the one that understands
directives.

Two implementation details are private and worth knowing about only because
they explain observable behaviour. The first is \code{in_comment_}, a
boolean member that records whether a block comment is currently open. It
persists across calls, which is what allows a file to be processed in
pieces, and it is also why the comment state of an inactive conditional
branch still has to be maintained (see below). The second is
\code{Preprocessor::substitute}, a private member function that performs
parameter substitution. It is unrelated to the free function
\code{dsl::substitute} of \chapref{ch:terms-and-reduction}, which operates
on terms and bindings; the two share a name and nothing else.

