Figure~\ref{fig:ch34-pipeline} shows the shape of the whole pass. The
source is first copied with backslash-newline sequences removed, so a
directive may be spread over several physical lines. The result is then
walked one line at a time. A line whose first non-blank character is a hash
is a directive; every other line is passed through the macro expander and
appended to the output.

There is one subtlety in the handling of lines that are not directives.
When the current conditional branch is \emph{inactive}, the line is still
fed through the macro expander, but with every macro name pre-disabled and
the result discarded. The purpose is not expansion at all; it is to keep
the block-comment state consistent. A \code{/*} inside a disabled branch
must still open a comment, or the text after the \texttt{\#endif} would be
misparsed.

\subsection{The macro expander}

The expander is a single left-to-right scan. At each position it asks what
kind of thing it is looking at, and it handles the cases in an order that
matters: comments and strings first, identifiers last.

\begin{pseudocode}[Expanding an identifier]
\textbf{function} expand\_impl (source, disabled, depth)\\
\quad if depth is greater than the limit then fail with a depth error\\
\quad out = empty text\\
\quad i = 0\\
\quad \textbf{while} i is less than the length of source\\
\qquad \textbf{if} a block comment is open\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad copy through the closing delimiter and clear the flag\\
\qquad \textbf{else if} the rest of the line is a line comment\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad copy the rest of the line and stop\\
\qquad \textbf{else if} source[i] opens a quoted string\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad copy the string verbatim, honouring backslash escapes\\
\qquad \textbf{else if} source[i] begins an identifier\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad read the whole identifier\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{if} it names a macro that is not disabled\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{if} the macro is function-like\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad scan the argument list\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad substitute the parameters\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad i = past the argument list\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad disable the macro name\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad out = out plus expand(replacement)\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad re-enable the macro name\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad disable the macro name\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad out = out plus expand(replacement)\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad re-enable the macro name\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad out = out plus the identifier\\
\qquad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad out = out plus source[i] and advance i\\
\quad \textbf{return} out
\end{pseudocode}

Two features of this scan are worth spelling out.

The first is the \emph{disabled} set. Before a macro's replacement is
expanded, the macro's own name is inserted into the set, and it is removed
again afterwards. A macro whose replacement mentions the macro itself ---
\texttt{\#define SELF SELF} --- therefore expands to the literal text
\code{SELF} and terminates. The same mechanism breaks mutual recursion:
with \texttt{\#define A B} and \texttt{\#define B A}, expanding \code{A}
yields \code{A}. The alternative, a special case for self-reference, would
handle only the trivial case; the set handles the general one.

The second is that the depth counter is checked on entry to
\code{expand_impl}. Each nested expansion increments it, so a long chain of
distinct macros eventually trips the limit and the preprocessor throws
\code{"macro expansion depth exceeded"}. Note that the disabled set will
usually stop a recursion first: \texttt{\#define X X} never reaches the
depth limit, because the disabled set stops it at the second expansion. The
limit exists for chains of \emph{distinct} macros.

\subsection{Function-like macros}

A function-like macro is invoked only if the next non-space character after
its name is a left parenthesis. If it is not, the name is copied through
unchanged, which is why \code{F} and \code{F (1)} behave differently when
\code{F} is defined with parameters: the first is not an invocation, the
second is.

The argument list is scanned with a nesting counter. A left parenthesis
increments the counter and a right parenthesis decrements it; a comma
splits an argument only when the counter is one. Quoted strings are skipped
over whole, so a comma inside a string does not split an argument and a
parenthesis inside a string does not affect the nesting. Each argument is
then trimmed of surrounding whitespace.

If the macro has no parameters and the argument list turns out to be a
single empty argument --- the text \code{F()} --- the argument list is
cleared, so that the macro is called with zero arguments rather than one.
Otherwise the argument count must equal the parameter count, or the
preprocessor throws \code{"macro argument count mismatch for X"}, where
\code{X} is the macro's name. An argument list that is never closed throws
\code{"unterminated macro invocation"}.

Arguments are macro-expanded before substitution, and the substituted
replacement is then macro-expanded in turn. That ordering is what makes
\code{SQUARE(NAME)} expand to \code{((world) * (world))} in the example
above: the argument \code{NAME} is expanded to \code{world} first, then
substituted into the body, and the body is expanded again.

The substitution itself is performed by the private
\code{Preprocessor::substitute}, which scans the replacement text and
replaces every identifier that matches a parameter name, skipping over
quoted strings. It is a plain textual substitution: the parameter value is
inserted verbatim, and it is the subsequent expansion pass that gives the
value its meaning.

\subsection{Conditionals}

The conditional directives are \texttt{\#if}, \texttt{\#ifdef}, \texttt{\#ifndef},
\texttt{\#elif}, \texttt{\#else} and \texttt{\#endif}. Their state is a stack of
branches, each of which records four booleans: whether the enclosing
branch was active when this one was pushed (\code{parent}), whether any
branch of this conditional has been taken (\code{taken}), whether this
branch is currently active (\code{active}), and whether an \texttt{\#else}
has been seen (\code{seen_else}).

\texttt{\#ifdef NAME} is true when \code{NAME} is in the macro table, and
\texttt{\#ifndef NAME} is its negation. Neither consults the disabled set,
so a macro that is currently being expanded still counts as defined.

\texttt{\#if} evaluates an expression with a small recursive-descent
evaluator. The grammar it accepts is:

\begin{itemize}
\item the negation \code{!};
\item parenthesised expressions;
\item \code{defined(X)} and \code{defined X};
\item identifiers, which are either macros or zero;
\item signed integer literals in decimal or hexadecimal;
\item the comparisons \code{==}, \code{!=}, \code{<=}, \code{>=},
\code{<} and \code{>};
\item conjunction \texttt{\&\&} and disjunction \texttt{\|\|}.
\end{itemize}

An identifier that names an object-like macro is evaluated by recursively
evaluating the macro's replacement as an expression, with the macro's own
name added to the disabled set. So \texttt{\#define VERSION 3} makes
\texttt{\#if VERSION >= 3} work, and \texttt{\#define FLAG (1 < 2)} works too.
An identifier that names a function-like macro, that names a macro which is
currently disabled, or that names nothing at all, evaluates to zero.

Integer literals may carry a sign and may be written in hexadecimal with a
\code{0x} prefix. There is no octal syntax.

The evaluator does not short-circuit: the right-hand side of \texttt{\&\&} and
\texttt{\|\|} is always evaluated. Since the only values are integers this
cannot cause a fault, but it does mean that a \texttt{\#if} expression
mentioning a macro with an undefined expansion will throw rather than
evaluate to false.

If the evaluator does not consume the whole expression text it throws
\texttt{"unsupported \#if expression"}. Other failures are
\texttt{"missing ')' in \#if"}, \code{"invalid defined expression"},
\texttt{"expected integer or identifier in \#if"} and
\texttt{"invalid \#if integer"}.

The errors thrown by the conditional machinery itself are
\code{"unmatched or duplicate conditional branch"} for a second
\texttt{\#else} or an \texttt{\#elif} after an \texttt{\#else},
\texttt{"unmatched \#endif"} for an \texttt{\#endif} with nothing open, and
\code{"unterminated preprocessor conditional"} for a conditional that is
still open when the source ends.

