Two details of the \texttt{\#define} directive are easy to get wrong. The
replacement text is not expanded when the macro is defined; it is expanded
each time the macro is used. This is the same lazy semantics as C, and it
is what allows a macro to be defined before the macros it mentions. The
second detail is that the parameter list must follow the name immediately.
\texttt{\#define F (x) body} defines an \emph{object-like} macro whose
replacement is the text \code{(x) body}, because the directive parser
looks at the very next character after the name to decide whether a
parameter list is present.

The \texttt{\#include} directive deserves a note of its own, because the
policy separation is most visible here. The directive's text is macro
expanded first, so \texttt{\#include HEADER} works if \code{HEADER} is a
macro whose value is a quoted path. The expanded text must then be a
quoted string or an angle-bracketed name; if it is neither, or if no
resolver has been installed, the preprocessor throws
\texttt{"\#include requires a resolver and a quoted or angle-bracket path"}.
The resolver receives the path with its delimiters removed --- the text
\code{"config.h"} and the text \code{<config.h>} both arrive as
\code{config.h} --- and must return the text to insert. That text is then
processed recursively through the whole pipeline, so an included file may
itself contain directives, conditionals and includes. The recursion is
bounded: a call at depth greater than \code{max_include_depth} throws
\code{"preprocessor include depth exceeded"}.

\section{The Lua bridge}

DSLtk ships exactly one script hook implementation, and it is compiled in
only when the user defines \code{DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA} and links against a
Lua library. The hook is a factory function that takes the user's
\code{lua_State} and returns a callable suitable for assignment to
\code{Preprocessor::script}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Installing the Lua hook. This fragment requires \code{DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA} and a Lua library; it is not part of the compiled program above.},label={lst:ch34-lua}]
#if defined(DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA)
#include <lua.hpp>
#include <iostream>

#include "DSLtk.hpp"

int
main ()
{
  lua_State *state = luaL_newstate ();
  dsl::Preprocessor pp;
  pp.script = dsl::lua_script_hook (state);

  // The body of the directive is handed to Lua and must return a string.
  std::cout << pp.process ("#lua return 'hello '\n");

  lua_close (state);
  return 0;
}
#endif
\end{lstlisting}

The hook behaves as follows. It rejects a null state, and it rejects any
language other than \code{"lua"}, with the message
\code{"invalid Lua script backend"}. It then saves the current stack top
with \code{lua_gettop} and installs a small RAII guard that restores it
with \code{lua_settop} when the call returns --- on success and on
failure alike, so that a failing script cannot leave junk on the caller's
stack. The code is compiled with \code{luaL_loadbuffer} and run with
\code{lua_pcall} expecting one result. If either step fails, the hook
throws \code{std::runtime_error} carrying the message from
\code{lua_tostring (state, -1)}. Finally, the result must be convertible
to a string; if it is not, the hook throws
\code{"Lua preprocessing must return a string"}.

The user owns the state. The hook does not create it, does not close it,
and does not keep a reference to it beyond the lifetime of the closure.

It is worth being explicit about what the Lua bridge is \emph{not}. It is
not a macro-expansion callback: there is no hook that is invoked when a
macro is expanded, and no way to compute a macro's replacement in Lua.
The only place a script can be invoked is a \texttt{\#lua} or \texttt{\#script}
directive, and its result is treated as ordinary text that is then
macro-expanded before being added to the output.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{warnbox}[The preprocessor is a text processor and not a parser]
The preprocessor has no notion of the language it is preprocessing. It
recognises comments and quoted strings because those are lexically
recognisable in almost every language, but it does not know about raw
strings, nested comments, or languages in which \texttt{\#} is an ordinary
character. A C++ raw string literal \texttt{R"(...\#define X...)"} will be
mangled, and a language that uses \texttt{\#} for something other than a
directive will be mangled too.
\end{warnbox}

The remaining limits are these.

\textbf{Directives are line-oriented.} A directive must be the first
non-blank thing on its line. There is no way to write a directive in the
middle of an expression, and there is no block form of \texttt{\#if}. The
only escape from the line rule is the backslash-newline continuation,
which is stripped before anything else happens, so a directive may be
spread over several lines.

\textbf{The preprocessor never sees the language.} Because it works on
text, it cannot know that a macro name occurring inside a comment-like
construct of the target language is not a comment. Languages with nested
block comments, or with a syntax in which \code{/*} appears inside a
string, will confuse it.

\textbf{There is no \texttt{\#warning} and no \texttt{\#pragma}.} Both fall
through to the unknown-directive error. This is a deliberate choice: a
preprocessor that silently ignored \texttt{\#pragma} would hide typos, and one
that implemented \texttt{\#warning} would need a warning channel that the
library does not otherwise have.

\textbf{Macros are not hygienic.} A macro's replacement is substituted
textually, so a parameter whose value happens to contain the name of
another macro will be expanded. There is no gensym mechanism and no
hygienic capture.

\textbf{There is no \texttt{\#} stringification and no token pasting.} The
replacement text is not re-tokenised, so there is no way to build a macro
name from its arguments.

\textbf{The script hook must return a string, and the result is
re-expanded.} A script that returns a number, a table or nothing at all
raises \code{"Lua preprocessing must return a string"}. The returned text
is macro-expanded before it reaches the output, so a script cannot be used
to inject text that must survive expansion.

\textbf{The comment state leaks between calls.} Because \code{in_comment_}
is a member and not a local, calling \code{process} on a fragment that ends
inside a block comment leaves the preprocessor in a state where the next
call will treat its first characters as comment text. There is no public
way to reset it.

\textbf{There is no way to report errors with a line number.} Every
diagnostic is a message with no position information. This is a significant
limitation for a tool that wants to point at the offending line.

\textbf{The output preserves the input's line structure only
approximately.} Directive lines are removed entirely, ordinary lines are
copied with their newline, and an included file's text is inserted at the
point of the \texttt{\#include}. The result is that the output's line
numbering does not necessarily correspond to the input's.

\section{Closing}

DSLtk's preprocessor is a small, sharp tool. It does macros properly ---
including function-like macros with nested argument lists, proper
substitution order and a termination guarantee --- and it does conditionals
with a real expression evaluator rather than a textual hack. What it
deliberately does not do is anything that would require the library to make
a policy decision on the user's behalf: it opens no files and it embeds no
scripting language. Both are delegated through a pair of function objects,
and the Lua bridge is the one implementation of the latter that ships.

The pattern --- a mechanism in the library, a policy in the embedding
application --- is the same pattern that the whole of DSLtk follows, and it
is why a header-only toolkit can offer an \code{IO} abstraction, a
visitor framework and a graph library without any of them dictating how the
host application should be structured.

The next chapter turns to the last of DSLtk's data structures: the
directed graph, with stable node identifiers, breadth-first and depth-first
traversal, topological sorting, shortest paths, strongly connected
components and a DOT exporter.
